Postcoital bleeding, Visible friable cervical mass, Foul-smelling vaginal discharge

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-008
Reason for Visit: Acute, heavy, and continuous vaginal bleeding with large clots following intercourse, accompanied by lightheadedness and presyncope.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute, massive postcoital hemorrhage with passage of large clots causing orthostatic dizziness. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess hemodynamic stability, apply local hemostatic measures to the cervix, and determine the need for immediate emergency transport to prevent hypovolemic shock before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I started bleeding profusely after sex a few hours ago, passing huge clots, and I feel like I am going to pass out when I stand.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began acutely three hours ago following intercourse. The patient reports sudden, heavy postcoital vaginal bleeding, passing multiple clots larger than a golf ball. This was preceded by a three-month history of irregular intermenstrual spotting and a foul-smelling, persistent, watery, and blood-tinged vaginal discharge which she had not investigated. She also reports a one-month history of deep, dull pelvic pain radiating down the posterior left leg. She denies prior cervical screening abnormalities, having not been screened in over a decade. Tonight, the bleeding has not slowed, and she reports significant dizziness upon standing.
Functional Status: Confined to bed; unable to mobilize safely due to orthostatic dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of acute hypovolemia and falls.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 98/60 mmHg | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Patient is pale, diaphoretic, and lying supine. Mildly anxious but cooperative.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft, non-tender, with no palpable masses. Speculum examination (performed carefully with adequate lighting) reveals a large, visible exophytic, extremely friable mass replacing the anterior lip of the cervix. Active, continuous, moderate arterial bleeding is noted from the indurated lesion. Large clots are present in the vaginal vault. No obvious unilateral lower extremity edema. Bimanual exam deferred to avoid exacerbating hemorrhage.
Environmental Observations: Blood-soaked towels present in the bathroom; partner is supportive but highly distressed.
Medication Reconciliation: No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Cervical Carcinoma with acute tumoral hemorrhage - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cervical Polyp - Unlikely due to the massive, exophytic, indurated nature of the lesion extending into the fornices, rather than a discrete, smooth pedunculated mass.

Vaginal Vault Laceration - Ruled out as the bleeding source is clearly visualized originating from the large cervical mass, with the vaginal walls intact.

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Unlikely given the lack of severe lower abdominal peritonism, absence of fever, and the presence of a distinct, actively bleeding gross structural lesion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Acute clinical instability)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with acute, heavy postcoital hemorrhage leading to objective signs of impending hemodynamic compromise (tachycardia, relative hypotension, orthostatic presyncope). Direct visualization confirms an actively bleeding, friable exophytic cervical mass highly suspicious for malignancy. The acute, ongoing blood loss and borderline hemodynamic instability required immediate after-hours intervention to attempt topical hemostasis and assess the need for urgent fluid resuscitation and hospital transfer. This critical intervention and stabilization could not be delayed until the next in-hours period without exposing the patient to the immediate risk of life-threatening hypovolemic shock.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral tranexamic acid 1g immediately. Packed the vaginal vault tightly with sterile gauze soaked in topical tranexamic acid to apply direct mechanical pressure to the friable mass. Advised the patient must remain strictly supine. Given the persistent tachycardia and active bleeding nature of the lesion, an emergency ambulance (000) was called for immediate transfer to the definitive gynaecological oncology center for formal packing, transfusion assessment, and biopsy.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain current blood pressure, remain strictly flat in bed without elevating the head; recognizing the immediate reduction in dizziness will reinforce strict compliance with the supine position while waiting for transport. To assist the paramedics, allow them to manage all movement during the transfer; understanding that exertion increases bleeding will encourage total physical rest.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Emergency Medical Services (Ambulance) activated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Handover provided directly to the receiving Emergency Department physician.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance already dispatched for immediate transfer to the Emergency Department due to high risk of massive hemorrhage and need for specialist intervention.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical appearance strongly suggests a significant cervical tumor; definitive diagnosis requires tissue biopsy in the hospital.
Clinical Course: The packing and medication will temporarily slow the bleeding, but definitive surgical or radiological intervention at the hospital is required tonight to secure the blood vessels.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to stand, walk, or remove the vaginal packing under any circumstances while waiting for the ambulance.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: There is a fragile growth on your cervix that has ruptured its blood vessels during intercourse. The gauze packing and medication act as a temporary plug to reduce the bleeding. However, because you have lost a significant amount of blood, you require immediate hospital care to safely manage this and investigate the growth.
Actionable Steps: Lie completely flat, try to remain calm to keep your heart rate down, and do not touch the internal packing.

